This code sets up a closed-loop DC motor speed control system for a miniature vehicle using quadrature encoders and a DRV8833 H-bridge. It uses the libgpiod library for GPIO control and implements a PI control loop every 100 ms with a 10 kHz PWM frequency. The code assumes 64 ticks per revolution and a 1:1 encoder-to-output-shaft ratio, targeting 50 RPM for both motors.

The code initializes the GPIO pins for the encoders, motor direction, and PWM signals, sets up callbacks for encoder tick counting and PWM generation, and enters a loop where it runs the PI control loop, checks for motor stalls, and prints the motor RPM and PWM duty cycle. The code handles SIGINT by stopping the motors and releasing resources.

Please note that this code does not include the actual hardware setup and wiring for the DRV8833 H-bridge and quadrature encoders. You will need to connect the motor, encoders, and other components according to the DRV8833 datasheet and your specific hardware setup.

To compile the code, use the following command:

```bash
gcc -Wall -o motor_control motor_control.c -lgpiod
```

Ensure you have the libgpiod development package installed:

```bash
sudo apt-get install libgpiod-dev
```

